#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365} :activity/id #uuid "3f795870-787b-5622-b078-947df7d3cd17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235 :topic/id #uuid "8b0ed26c-25c0-57a8-8d1c-6baa9735f5e3" :topic/math-academy-id 6373} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222492 :tutorial/content "We have seen how, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the current point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhile the implicit Euler method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the next point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nBoth have their advantages, but is there a way to combine them into one numerical method, applying the slope at both the current point and next point to update $y?$\n\nYes, there is! One such method is the trapezoidal method, which takes the *average slope in the interval between $x$ and $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$*\n\nMore formally, the **trapezoidal method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nLike the implicit Euler method, the trapezoidal method is an implicit numerical method. To apply the method, we substitute the formula for $\\Delta y$ into the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(f(x,y)+f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve the resulting implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$ directly, rather than explicitly calculating the value of $\\Delta y$ beforehand.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on IVPs whose slope function is linear in $y,$ i.e., of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can simply solve the implicit equation for $y_\\text{new},$ as demonstrated in the following worked example.\n\nFor more complex slope functions, we require a root-finding method, such as Newton's method, to solve the implicit equation iteratively. We'll cover this in a later lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1419612-b51a-5051-9478-0b5686cac1cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14490 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fc3f4329-6227-53b8-a848-08d6504d86a7" :step/math-academy-id 600181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222493 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nLet's use the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, in general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+1+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 = 1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 + 3\\cdot2 + 2= 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 9.$ We can view our approximation made by the trapezoidal method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a233830a75e76788ed9c4a5436ecb237aad6257591f0e20768c796195dca8eb2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c20c0100-eb93-50ee-b684-c56f4c9ec02d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14491 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "30729e81-2455-57d6-9939-77e4c4541d91" :step/math-academy-id 600182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221179 :question/id #uuid "62a1e90b-05a5-5828-abe0-fa5cbb4c1c1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308649}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "e6615bd4-7ab0-53c0-94f6-6ac2b5d6f381" :step/math-academy-id 600183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221178 :question/id #uuid "5399454b-0691-5cb3-884f-26af4ca52f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-21671"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372738}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "c9aae869-52a0-5af1-bbbd-93e447dc18db" :step/math-academy-id 600186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221180 :question/id #uuid "d387bb78-9df1-5551-9e1e-5a4745c5b731" :question/math-academy-id "e-21673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372753}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "250a7a8e-0521-5bf0-ad50-c0c51ae3eba8" :step/math-academy-id 600189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222532 :tutorial/content "Once the first iteration of a trapezoidal method implementation is complete, we can continue applying the same general update formula iteratively.\n\nIn our previous example, we used one iteration of the trapezoidal method on the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2,\n$$\n\nusing the general formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = x + x_\\text{new} + 3y + 2\n$$\n\nto approximate the value $y(1)\\approx9.$ Let's now approximate the value of $y(2)$ with one more step.\n\nSince the step size $\\Delta x = 1$ remains the same, the update formula derived in the previous step still applies.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have $x=1$ and $y=9.$ Hence, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 1+1=2,$ and the new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+2+3\\cdot 9+2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 32.$ We can visualize this second step as follows:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9752c16b15ebcfcfa214c775e99fb68139a1dfa9364a5f552fbf676d7953f7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "31f14503-26a9-5e09-b9cf-10a636f52db7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14898 :tutorial/title "Multiple Iterations of the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "b71ca947-77a6-59f8-9d4b-6ed77ec28832" :step/math-academy-id 608074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369}}] :activity/title "The Trapezoidal Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221465}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180341} :activity/id #uuid "90074413-5fe3-5632-8eb0-3e0dc053df00" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/id #uuid "9a5670e8-43ef-54b7-8f4b-81842fc55f1c" :topic/math-academy-id 3715} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180787 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water, and $C$ is a curve that allows water to flow through it (like a fishing net). \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol C$** is a scalar number that tells us how much water is escaping the region bounded by $C.$ \n\nWe have three possible scenarios: \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n![](images/6f/6ff4d006d3fbf4a434f35a13c8591fc166ff6c74adf2b7969752b7541e486df2.png)\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/38/3812148e855a84e3233316acc8bbf59bd7cd802a874c9e4807c6efd7ca6754cf.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\nThe following vector field has zero flux across $C$ because the amount of water flowing in through $C$ equals the amount of water flowing out through $C.$\n\n![](images/56/56aef4f217e9981755826745a97e8389d86b49518ffd7d3ebc75e5ee2dc28f8b.png)\n\nThe following two vector fields also have a zero flux across $C$ because the water flow is tangential to $C$ at every point along $C.$\n\n![](images/63/637677324152af3d428e405c89025f35dbe5d71e2e8f0a0797ef3aca30bae7e1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ecd8591f-8e5b-50b2-91a1-4f15bd97b2c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9521 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e80fc64-e097-5e8d-a35c-056416216619" :step/math-academy-id 184955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180788 :tutorial/content "If $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ The symbol $\\oint\\limits$ means integration over the closed curve.\n\nIt's helpful to spend some time understanding where this definition comes from, so let's derive it using the closed curve $C$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6dfe8a071798c93f19507808596b7d32af90367b2666643d092a7c50afe5e929.png)\n\nFirst, we pick a point $P$ on $C.$ At this point, we draw the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the outward-pointing normal vector $\\mathbf n$ to $C.$\n\n![](images/49/498e1afbaac8bc8297213eae09cc8bf8742b29f45dda1dfafff868c67ebb9de9.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P.$ Now, $\\mathbf F$ is made up of two components:\n\n- a component parallel to $\\mathbf n,$ which contributes to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ and\n\n- a component perpendicular to $\\mathbf n,$ which does *not* contribute to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\nWe're only interested in the contribution made to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ This is given by the scalar projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac3aae49f372908f2f9aeb53e3fc98bff117846fca05a8c696009e443390811c.png)\n\nTherefore, the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}F & =\\frac{F\\cdot n}{||n||}=F\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above represents the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at a single point only. Therefore, to calculate the total net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ we sum (integrate) this expression over the entire curve. This gives\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a328d4d8-bfe8-50eb-8bbd-281b3bb07afe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9522 :tutorial/title "Constructing an Integral Formula for the Flux"} :step/id #uuid "9dac0bb5-3a03-56c0-a614-dc9f045cb3e3" :step/math-academy-id 184956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180789 :tutorial/content "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "36b855d8-9994-5c22-bbc4-e28065875f84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9523 :tutorial/title "Orientation-Independence"} :step/id #uuid "a000a04f-fdd8-5b25-9b23-e59a14a3592d" :step/math-academy-id 184957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180344}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179482 :question/id #uuid "c427d9b4-2944-5edc-8e19-363de348d23d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15147"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "4f8760a0-8c8e-5ec6-9492-0ecc32789da6" :step/math-academy-id 184958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180345}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180790 :tutorial/content "To calculate the flux of a vector field over a smooth, closed curve, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nIf $C=C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_n$ is piecewise smooth, we have to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over each smooth face. That is,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+ \\cdots +\n\\int\\limits_{C_n} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ on $C_i.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf i + (y-x)^3\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C,$ we sum the fluxes of $\\mathbf F$ over each side:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to compute these integrals, let's construct the flux integral across the side $C_4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe side $C_4$ and its outward-pointing unit normal are shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06fd5a2ecbd709e8e835f2a57ef3ada772526366d51e5e16474d9b8635500e8e.png)\n\nSince this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = -\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i+(y-x)^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i)\\cdot (-j)+((y-x)^{3}\\,j))\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}0-(y-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(x-y)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing a similar procedure for the other sides, we can construct the full expression for the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "472c362c-27a5-5041-90c3-4533c63b736d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9524 :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux"} :step/id #uuid "ab771459-c3a6-5f4b-9e75-cfa3f13b9add" :step/math-academy-id 184961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179483 :question/id #uuid "15426c37-1508-5407-ab49-b26f8761795c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420913}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"} :step/id #uuid "2c288a88-997c-5ef1-98ff-24092e7506f9" :step/math-academy-id 184962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180791 :tutorial/content "Calculating the flux of a vector field across a rectangle or square requires computing four flux integrals. However, we can often simplify our calculations if the vector field displays symmetry.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Let's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65a8c37c64580f7d1f41f32722f20dfee2b8fd98333920a0feae781ea8d9541e.png)\n\nSince the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ is parallel to $C_2$ and $C_4,$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is easy to verify, since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}-5\\,i\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand similarly for the integral along $C_4.$ \n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through $C_1$ will cancel with the net outflow through $C_3.$ That is\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n-\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53e768e4-a3bb-5b89-83b9-a97e9ff4a921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9525 :tutorial/title "Using  Symmetry to Simplify Flux Calculations"} :step/id #uuid "5c82115b-5b07-5765-b4a2-64382478cffb" :step/math-academy-id 184965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484 :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420917}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "3b16b4e1-26b3-5b3f-a7bb-3a7ea8b7f740" :step/math-academy-id 184966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179485 :question/id #uuid "3f592794-e0e9-529c-9040-7b2806de2380" :question/math-academy-id "e-15150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421470}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"} :step/id #uuid "ff88b373-d7aa-57f4-97d5-9266bed9219b" :step/math-academy-id 184969}] :activity/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179866}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453} :activity/id #uuid "4cb8dd70-6479-5b35-b723-3890d781528c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048388 :topic/id #uuid "9b5fc10c-ada7-55cd-8d40-6c4729395195" :topic/math-academy-id 3693} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179551 :question/id #uuid "055422e3-fb8a-5579-ab97-b946d5687e30" :question/math-academy-id "e-9052"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050903}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365371}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"} :step/id #uuid "ec16918e-d414-592f-ad99-94fed4da6b99" :step/math-academy-id 33784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179552 :question/id #uuid "a4d988d6-4181-588c-8e11-02a96554a76a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365329}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"} :step/id #uuid "c3cafc43-ac5d-5524-9bd0-e3b82ec62fd4" :step/math-academy-id 33785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180703 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate a particle's velocity and displacement when its acceleration is a function of *displacement.* In this lesson, we'll use this knowledge to compute various properties of a particle in motion.\n\nSuppose a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. Its displacement from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ and its acceleration is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$\n\n![](images/52/5222b12a7f08a33dc981ac95bd717cce945e1d2e3565fb3631f108fd0f307a12.png)\n\nFurthermore, suppose $P$ has velocity $2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $\\sqrt{3}\\,\\rm{m}.$\n\nLet's use this information to determine at which values of $x$ the particle is instantaneously at rest.\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2x.$$\n\nUsing the fact that $$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),$$ we can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x.$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int 2x \\:\\textrm{d}x$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2+ C.$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=\\sqrt 3\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2-1.$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ We find the displacement of the particle when $P$ is instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\,m\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcdbb7a7-457c-5386-9a73-ae4d9da40f89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5769 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "28c57294-3b4c-56e0-8cac-331a28850e07" :step/math-academy-id 35399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179577 :question/id #uuid "58325462-3a69-575e-bdfb-31db815b0d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365316}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "1a2b4d25-22b2-5303-9d34-924730dd694c" :step/math-academy-id 35400}] :activity/title "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179847}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167843} :activity/id #uuid "be95ee71-ebc4-546c-a738-6a6db824342c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395 :topic/id #uuid "9bb55214-36f6-5124-91d2-24ff1cb653aa" :topic/math-academy-id 3242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167997 :tutorial/content "Consider a *decoupled* linear system of ODEs\n![](images/3f/3f7a114fa75100f8cd3ccf72822758e668f3a6a0824ad5f5437ebef138928fb5.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we consider only decoupled systems with nonzero eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8ed089264b88bdcf4018f44db6dade448063b56a6ded558fb74d7c2ca61c0de.svg) so the origin is the equilibrium point we will classify.\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues for a diagonal matrix are its diagonal entries, ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) \nThe corresponding eigenvectors are parallel to the coordinate axes:\n![](images/83/8337ebfc4aae4b8e97438cdba58872082bdc8965932659fdf2f8ff9cf061b375.svg)\n\nSo, the general solution of the system is given by\n![](images/fc/fcb5c1cea49dfe7aa1058f64d859a8b47c363f7f6195bf68056b18f1fa6d37f2.svg)\n\nNotice that if a solution starts on one of the coordinate axes, it remains on that axis. These are called **straight-line solutions**:\n\n- On the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis (![](images/8d/8da7ef7a61d4c5af4d28575fcd263714cf3bda8e7d94985bd4bea5c03eb2d53f.svg)): \n![](images/aa/aa9cd3c49609ee6d09a61c9c31438e4297620feedfda1073fbc55c08e7c53d01.svg)\n\n- On the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis (![](images/f2/f271ee76900762067d886b30871a9ea7538ae69318ad4514e5b39cfdce12644b.svg)):\n![](images/50/509154a662b215d445b282396a8f43828e582d43282467a0da73843ade7ec5d8.svg)\n\nThe stability of these solutions as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the *signs* of the eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) We will explore the three main stability cases in the upcoming examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "3ab2b715-abcc-511c-bbda-a2664d39efda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14436 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a60bbebf-7d50-57b0-b823-f5cde68f6ecb" :step/math-academy-id 588112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168002 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0.$ \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/75/75e7a0e16141db2a8da210c1ab30fc7328ccc98f861afec4db69d951ff3c9121.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/28/280e99a308962d43d1060be7100e294623d3bc3bfbe0579ab61d817695ad9e45.svg) Since $\\lambda_2>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity. \n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "302544e8-9eeb-5087-ae71-7143ffcbad9f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14460 :tutorial/title "Both Positive Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "752fcd7d-e772-51d0-84f5-e0724d157464" :step/math-academy-id 588113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168003 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n![](images/3e/3e1a6630e46ba96ca21946d9feedc452d8416fe5bac17d5e8275c41c489d19ea.svg)\nwhere ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg)\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/a1/a12ab1d3c69a589aa8de66fe8d14ed19b2938a9ef5591d87860e101a95afdc55.svg) Since ![](images/3d/3db8d2103adafd2be29df482295b07ef63576be6f152793463e034e808725edf.svg) we have that ![](images/bb/bb6223675110a379d33203ff637afaa6aefd3edf507b5a93386c608aa7aafca5.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/aa/aae0f8015e0a575ca1e1fdbb37d011cbf78574649e3c9874163c3f14f1ca9929.svg) Since ![](images/23/23d0a8291d49cc1d2f0c6cff735a4d5214c2d9079dca67d803846af9b063358f.svg) we have that ![](images/69/69c464920e94e968ac5b2fe07f52d5e8a5e694f0fab6c9bfdcfd1b359a82e89e.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/11/119ac25f56cfb326a9eb39eaf63c41e8497147c80b14da3a38e557e8aeb4f905.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "98c957b9-817c-5bdd-9e7a-9680641d5c55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14461 :tutorial/title "Both Negative Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "4ed4db19-c2d0-5c82-a414-637905d1a91d" :step/math-academy-id 588114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166536 :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420022}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"} :step/id #uuid "0849e9c4-6231-5416-bf0c-d15d2ba66f0e" :step/math-academy-id 588115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166537 :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420024}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"} :step/id #uuid "d06d3f5b-d8b3-5195-a3ad-20d231c0a7e8" :step/math-academy-id 588118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168001 :tutorial/content "For a decoupled system\n![](images/df/df904ec5214399987c94f99323d3c3c4c06d9e19242c0d580574488011475acc.svg)\nwe know the *signs* of ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) tell us the type of the equilibrium (sink/source/saddle). But how can we determine the shape of trajectories in cases when the origin is a source (![](images/bb/bb6302eab08feb0614c62d4ea8012676675715a469dd30a7321ec228ff7ef4e9.svg)) or a sink (![](images/be/be8860e40a1c229262d3bf3375732f4b4583cad7aaf63f3a17e871bbd5a51abb.svg))?\n\nTo do this, we can compare the rates of change of ![](images/f7/f7dbfa03976240534a1b57f2cc59c474401a0d9524bc5f627736da01a0d775c1.svg) and ![](images/cc/cc436bde605f3822acb66d36528ba42a812a50fdbea361b31f0dbd0cba61d810.svg) by looking at the slope of the tangent line to the trajectory, ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg) Assuming ![](images/37/371aec72d3e326e3e52fbd1a1cfc06be06b33e3054e60b8c286a9fcbac39141e.svg) we have:\n![](images/be/be21baf0c518d85b105b0f569382fbc47075c268847437046b042db36a9b4b8a.svg)\n\nAs we can see, the long-term behavior of the slope as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the sign of the exponent, ![](images/7c/7cde6653feb562f0d136150bffa6f2925af0c7dbcb9ede5c7f1b3e0f10f3796d.svg)\n\nThis comparison is governed by which eigenvalue is larger. We call the larger eigenvalue (the one that is more positive, or less negative) the **dominant eigenvalue**.\n\nIn the next slides, we will analyze the source and sink cases separately." :tutorial/id #uuid "406ac21a-bfe3-5af2-bc34-8bb09ae11870" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14456 :tutorial/title "Dominant Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "7d22549d-343c-5238-823d-0fdd3d6b11cf" :step/math-academy-id 588121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420026}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "edbdd357-26e5-5671-9724-72c90fe9512d" :step/math-academy-id 588122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167850}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168004 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n![](images/e1/e1efdd0b8c037c01450042384ce3bac663d0b549fa9557d0db09b692754cab0f.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fd81402-072b-5676-9cac-f53af20f3174" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14462 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "b8abcadd-58fc-5f3e-a1a9-f6f32aaa7405" :step/math-academy-id 588125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166540 :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420025}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "6cadc020-1f0c-5e3d-88b7-c3977e63cc74" :step/math-academy-id 588126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168020 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$ (the opposite case, where $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2>0,$ is similar). \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/4d/4dc3aaf0f7dfd85070df84c13abe2cf02d85a704167569b3d3a76f2b58178c5b.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin. This is called an **unstable axis**.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/76/76f6b9bbcdff2c87295fb0cd14dbdbbaa5e6c75eeb49c1cc158a4e8bd3da059d.svg) Since $\\lambda_2<0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin. This is called a **stable axis**.\n\nBecause one axis is stable and the other is unstable, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **saddle point**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), while\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory bends from the stable direction and then peels away along the unstable direction.\n\n![](images/f2/f2538a59050e604d85fd9726e688cfbd2a5c9bdff4d15dd5ed465ed04f7290b4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8fe29b2e-2a4a-5a2d-aaea-49ed3c5a7c1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14565 :tutorial/title "One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "7cfc4842-67bf-5d76-b1c9-2607d80a8a6e" :step/math-academy-id 588184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168057 :tutorial/content "Let's analyze the case where the origin is a source, with ![](images/b8/b89d2dea375e40cafca8a025742ea1ba94b01ff3dd5926dc21b62444847e237f.svg)  For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories move away from the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/3c/3cda8c604691cfc8889d64007bce911c2ea6bd3ba06a7a14cb3adef0cacbccfc.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) In this case:\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/a5/a5bd23277d5f3a652a992839592d09551cd2278773b0f92cbdc06b247f69dc64.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) In this case:\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nas ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) This means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories move away from the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move away from the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/28/286b72736b47e9d318f585fc0ef0ae69e4150914c3ca7a8981d236d08305a6a5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "534a4770-a0a6-5b07-a36e-0bc08f985630" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14895 :tutorial/title "Unstable Node (Source)"} :step/id #uuid "ee8907e9-ae68-5313-b486-9d33968f5dc9" :step/math-academy-id 608071 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168058 :tutorial/content "Now, let's analyze the case where the origin is a sink, with ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg) For a decoupled system with solutions ![](images/6b/6b0666459fb6515324b663cc73f0e9327d845f40fd7772aa8850a4f14b55239d.svg) and ![](images/e7/e78f66d899f375fcf9894ab58968f92123556325139411dd278712df59c7e0ce.svg) trajectories approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/48/481eb665d12add01a80a4646ec2762c89c9a543bf87b959b246d469a450eca6d.svg) then ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/27/27aba369b910849246f3c1c32106bca7e1e83a6223410a2fdc233fdea0a7367b.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/b6/b6ee7ac8d56a4a4a1cdfb29948285c86491fc884911de538b875420bcabf1b55.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as trajectories approach the origin.\n\n- If ![](images/80/80105ab4e61c0e2a59d11da4b534753b3c811b424f1e0b82704c6e28faecf9b8.svg) then ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) is the *dominant eigenvalue* and ![](images/b0/b0dbb3708b2ff42502115d98637093d3b96d1f3f4ee4f9393046d0c3396068a6.svg) As ![](images/37/370259707c15937e566c781bb8f7aaf39643a5c059b246f2cec5477528eee994.svg)\n![](images/fd/fd6de4580c33c90dcadc8599e19917522b63327c668e8fec6856eec61c7ba3a3.svg)\nThis means the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as trajectories approach the origin.\n\nNotice that in both cases, trajectories become parallel to the axis corresponding to the *dominant eigenvalue* as they move toward the origin (the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis on the left, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis on the right).\n\n![](images/b6/b665d5328ae362ada2208da2583fd90247078490145426072455d43f8d36227f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b95d431-e3ab-5635-b641-a019d23397b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14896 :tutorial/title "Stable Node (Sink)"} :step/id #uuid "78ab8590-2a54-5795-87c0-331930a7d959" :step/math-academy-id 608072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167849}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167013}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193613} :activity/id #uuid "e3d33137-c31a-567d-baff-3e933e4f2403" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048532 :topic/id #uuid "a7907bdb-038e-5baa-a4ae-df7b362e291b" :topic/math-academy-id 4100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192473 :question/id #uuid "3f141210-8194-5346-9e2a-c47f98bb3443" :question/math-academy-id "e-10840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048744}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421866}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "d9a24908-03a6-51d6-b5f1-3155cc2dccaf" :step/math-academy-id 41081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193862 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIt is common for the set $S$ of a continuous random variable $X$ to be an infinite interval. Therefore, we often need to evaluate improper integrals when determining whether a function is a valid pdf and to compute probabilities associated with such a random variable.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following function:\n\n![](images/38/38ea105f5dd0cea7784bf73f814e7161b90cfd224eb099084cc33b6539d95fdd.svg)\n\nIs $f(x)$ a valid probability density function? Let's start by sketching its graph:\n\n![](images/35/3559a998491a004c159683aaf0946bb4fdf87f4afe873fdc93235e840b4b0ac8.png)\n\nClearly, $f(x)\\geq 0$ for all $x,$ so this function satisfies the first condition.\n\nTo check that $f(x)$ satisfies the second condition, we need to compute the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_S f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-\\infty }]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $f(x)$ satisfies both conditions, we conclude that $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f9c0835e-afeb-5f5c-b296-1e37ad68d3fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6775 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "00b1a7d3-74fb-5434-befa-306fc78d3a8a" :step/math-academy-id 41119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192474 :question/id #uuid "d40a67bd-7d98-58fb-b4a1-7a556e91556a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10851"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408037}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"} :step/id #uuid "cccafa59-dc36-5d28-96de-c705cc5a1797" :step/math-academy-id 41121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192529 :question/id #uuid "5f988175-cdd9-5ddf-94b5-3b995dc75fce" :question/math-academy-id "e-12329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408038}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"} :step/id #uuid "369bab2a-0778-58cb-b362-2b26468c8bd0" :step/math-academy-id 44173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193614}}] :activity/title "Continuous Random Variables Over Infinite Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192925}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421869} :activity/id #uuid "2dc4dabc-aedf-5892-921f-490e984369c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048532 :topic/id #uuid "a7907bdb-038e-5baa-a4ae-df7b362e291b" :topic/math-academy-id 4100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6023af0f-b4dd-541f-b0d2-9db3f518cd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260707} {:answer/id #uuid "d02f4e02-7b6c-5777-aee2-33c54a892f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261714} {:answer/id #uuid "d6172bf6-5ae9-5323-b13a-c469aa93b4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261758} {:answer/id #uuid "ea772249-1b9e-54d7-aff3-07375a0afdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261933} {:answer/id #uuid "f5989cb2-1e59-5e2c-9106-aaed97e1d35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260707} :answer-field/id #uuid "249bf36d-1590-59fa-bf5d-ae59c8724944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4715a163-98e1-5514-9f6b-3d69d82eb15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126028" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(4<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}]}_{4}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01b175bd-e667-5a75-b3df-02b40ee2e81b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28308daa-8ddb-545e-8465-8f0a73db0af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421855} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8aadca-e9d8-5956-bfd5-24321e9b6037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421857} {:answer/id #uuid "a495ea88-69a7-52f5-b2d7-6986e9a706f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421858} {:answer/id #uuid "c724da1c-2c42-5050-aba4-21fa7939277a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421859} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dbe1c3-a9e4-5566-b39f-c692e5091488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3bf738-9bc7-5e68-a507-db9436929f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "942a9198-9a9b-5910-a327-96717799bf91" :question/math-academy-id "q-87226" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =P(3\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}]}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1be2574c-a3ce-5ec0-bb9f-88e4dc8755a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146aee1a-528b-5bbf-a4b6-4fbf2f271f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421853} {:answer/id #uuid "27715182-2cb9-52c2-a080-36df60c28e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421854} {:answer/id #uuid "7e007eff-0deb-57f5-a6ba-37ea62edbf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421856} {:answer/id #uuid "e008e939-3bb6-57fd-a8f3-41e658e78a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421860} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c19772-5317-5a44-8357-af32f4360941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421853} :answer-field/id #uuid "55603476-e287-5779-991f-12f57c478278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "168818a7-6140-5751-9382-8a48afe2a16e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87221" :question/problem "Compute $P(X<-5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{4x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-5) & =P(-\\infty <X<-5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}4{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{4x}]}_{-\\infty }^{-5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe10f7a6-87e1-5126-8846-1670d19ab5f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421868}}] :activity/title "Continuous Random Variables Over Infinite Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421852}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909} :activity/id #uuid "029bded9-b237-543e-8c70-78a922e3ce4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173163 :tutorial/content "A  plane curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r:[a,b]\\to \\Bbb R^2\n$$\n\nis said to be **simple** if it doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints. Notice that  this means that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_1)\\neq \\mathbf r(t_2), \\qquad \na < t_1< t_2 < b.\n$$\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is simple while curve II is not simple.\n\n![](images/7b/7be1bc23d37c187a642d51917d6acb846147059bc6e9978d6ccd4ed35eed8794.png)\n\nA  plane curve is said to be **closed** if its terminal point coincides with its initial point, that is, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(b)= \\mathbf r(a).\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b21a6e638cb8b0bae8967ba142fbfacb14349f34ff80d9359643e03ea1c1f83.png)\n\nFor example, curve III shown below is closed, while curve IV is not:\n\n![](images/85/8507fb039da42f37307ca3994cfb5ad66b288fb28d0cfd1a1a179acd2f83ae5e.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Curve III is an example of both a closed and simple curve. Notice that an example of a closed but *not* simple curve looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86dcd1dc864a2f0e2ceade072ba230b8fb4fe8923189a7eed2964f6f66a67651.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "72ccdc89-4807-5abb-9f69-7e017f195c3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5100 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bafdfe3-0f81-5668-ad79-241cf2fc2b91" :step/math-academy-id 32008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171853 :question/id #uuid "0bf9999b-16dd-567b-9e52-e80a1b0c6a38" :question/math-academy-id "e-8675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "325cfa6e-a3ff-51f3-9667-95e4ad86ac4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395507}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simple Curves"} :step/id #uuid "8a0f9d7f-ab53-56ab-bdd3-8439d8f9ce7a" :step/math-academy-id 32200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171854 :question/id #uuid "ee0133c9-056e-5595-b407-8f8f00c49823" :question/math-academy-id "e-8676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca16ec8-183d-5e2e-9715-24107f8aa8c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418066}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b500b6d6-4ad6-5209-ba0d-8f0be8ba230d" :step/math-academy-id 32201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171855 :question/id #uuid "1b8cbd6a-e306-5092-a402-6a0da61c5ec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418065}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "01467366-cedf-5b66-ab24-41950e76376f" :step/math-academy-id 32202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173171 :tutorial/content "A very important (although very hard to prove) result in mathematics states the following:\n\nAny simple and closed curve divides the plane into exactly two regions, one inside the curve and one outside.\n\nBased on this fact, we can define the **orientation** of a simple closed curve as follows:\n\nA simple closed curve is **positively oriented** if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is **negatively oriented**.\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is positively oriented, while curve II is negatively oriented:\n\n![](images/95/9527dd8bdfd979a1d521ccf945e84ef9b557d67e3d89ce8f5e4f7bb8d287ee8c.png)\n\nAn alternative convenient way of looking at simple oriented curves is that a curve is positively oriented if it is traversed *counterclockwise* and it is negatively oriented if it is traversed *clockwise*." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2c2591-a914-53a3-9f60-6e7335d049f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5165 :tutorial/title "Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "2b76b9e7-6d5f-584c-837f-bd003bd3c835" :step/math-academy-id 32465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918}}] :activity/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172178}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418068} :activity/id #uuid "efd4467a-4377-5bbc-a79e-4cb35382c6af" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fb86e1-67a1-5051-8581-e7df8f55db54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9e/9e85303ac91d084f055bce35c874eaaab191a44602ce9223e5fda774b8491183.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411684} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacc02f-cc03-57e3-a071-b880e2b112dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/86d0a572024ecdbb9ea9d73b20db0710edb26313e5834b78ad48b494b1f09f69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411726} {:answer/id #uuid "cadeec58-fb13-573b-93e4-7beb0073d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/71c3bcf20ac5e029c6e0033c0e69d7de04b5285bc68d5b7711c892d27d6eef9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411788} {:answer/id #uuid "db57e4b9-2310-5420-bc1a-5ace58a2c6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/47/47497b397779489e0685a201a792a36ea2e2755c299063c61403087679442971.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411800} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f258c5-797c-5c27-9c31-df5ed6f9b74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c6/c6fe4fef71ac41a00491fec02b6a38722dbe2549d3f729fbe08cdf20464ebda2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411800} :answer-field/id #uuid "8103638e-24d6-587a-8696-759f9ac5cf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71602977-82c1-5c53-87d8-1a470298d861" :question/math-academy-id "q-98330" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is simple, closed, and positively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise. \n\nAmong the given options, the only positively oriented simple closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/47/47497b397779489e0685a201a792a36ea2e2755c299063c61403087679442971.png)"} :step/id #uuid "07c058c1-979f-5403-be26-08111075c178" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b8832f-f459-53a5-bebf-7fce914129df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361085} {:answer/id #uuid "6d83564b-c398-53bd-bfb5-edc2ccbb4a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361325} {:answer/id #uuid "8b48145e-5103-5488-9198-266b70ef8cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361451} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ee88e8-8bfe-59fa-ba06-abba60542584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361744} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d37f94-5695-59cd-99be-4cf97c4ae5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361085} :answer-field/id #uuid "85184abf-390f-5ccf-a8a9-0834b6bdbc7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8658a678-c6ba-5ca2-b63b-0885c74173b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97928" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b0ced561cd98f9cf81c7de42bee7411a39595a9c59f2edaad0121d712dad157.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2696ee1a-694a-5753-8a6f-3768b6b0920b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eeddbc3-0c2b-51c9-a0c9-e864cef13cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cd/cd1f754fa00d1af2d8901ecc4c0cac434afb433474685fe7777d74f1703bafb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361045} {:answer/id #uuid "59405ea1-d578-52de-82ff-d38f4fd2dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2d352cf134fa7aa740d42f1de4e731cf9f53e26341a4ee3fefae809db0c8a3f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361243} {:answer/id #uuid "617af790-b042-53c8-b62b-12dee18a22f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/58efe73124a5f0fa8824ea05d118ea92ea1b30cf950f740c9c9b5376650044bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361274} {:answer/id #uuid "a263b79a-9702-5011-a289-351b7e5a3912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c7c5f96c6f006efc4c77fbb9e5c8d903752e985a0128b870904e41a7454d734.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361562} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e25dc6-1d69-57f8-90f2-fb4dc5deb23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/55/5506e37cb76f6a91e556420f402aff1a8a4aeffe713ab98f92dc6854c41a0752.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca67f3e-9ac1-55cf-8b2b-336c11770793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec48ee0a-34ee-5675-ada5-b482eb70f371" :question/math-academy-id "q-98323" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is *not* closed?" :question/worked-solution "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/55/5506e37cb76f6a91e556420f402aff1a8a4aeffe713ab98f92dc6854c41a0752.png)"} :step/id #uuid "2cbff5dc-ba2c-5c03-b7e5-c598ee76979a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19015d56-d409-5e38-9d72-d0a571908ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e7/e7e2c6fcda97890e06ae12c7f2e175e0c0c75bdcbc66cbd82d9802f92449d6e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418053} {:answer/id #uuid "44cb249c-78ea-5c88-8794-19ba3bd7aadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/22/22a50051d5281880b40c0960c2de28ce077e836a4bf1c84193d13154ff25f6a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418057} {:answer/id #uuid "87035970-291d-57cf-99b2-27ae082ac3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1ea7160d343a681d7a81e93eb755c252a17186fe85e66ad83d63c51d179f433b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418060} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd22568-357c-5669-9cba-b0e0ee0cd4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1f/1fccb402189374def1b1a7c52aabd690364335ee373fb7b6c68f467c085cf585.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc32805-5477-58a9-9d2b-3ec91327f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/44/44e7444bde45c40f830ea4ccac708488cbb90a9595cb184dcdc4f5011ff66f58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418057} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fd376f-ce32-577b-9dd3-21c563e97b1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3651fdc-2d8e-55e9-b5a9-f7f59a5112bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-98319" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is *not* closed?" :question/worked-solution "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/22/22a50051d5281880b40c0960c2de28ce077e836a4bf1c84193d13154ff25f6a5.png)"} :step/id #uuid "9c643061-7cf9-5282-9130-02e247ef91c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b798def-9f4c-5c78-939d-4d3fa7b155b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418054} {:answer/id #uuid "36a1e53d-4736-58e7-9183-e39dca9e0ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418055} {:answer/id #uuid "4305aa10-6b9d-5a8d-b5e0-73d08076c6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418056} {:answer/id #uuid "64b20a39-4422-555e-853c-addaea9024df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418058} {:answer/id #uuid "6db79444-4f16-52ba-8432-cedcce75ea2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418054} :answer-field/id #uuid "8230f704-b679-555a-b3ea-6455f032cf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6641b4f-e396-5ff5-8410-bd9f53c4e8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98339" :question/problem "![](images/98/9836234d763ccb3da6b9ac0360dcb3662cacc5fc90d5ecc2dee20bf29ce0c77d.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is neither simple nor closed.\n\n- Curve II is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "bba698a5-c622-5631-9486-7b254dcdc5b9"}] :activity/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418052}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406} :activity/id #uuid "92d0371b-d4af-52c0-a1d3-83d95f7cf2d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048912 :topic/id #uuid "cbc6dbdd-cff6-5796-8970-556d22f811ac" :topic/math-academy-id 6397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222483 :tutorial/content "For a first-order initial value problem, we have already seen ways to approximate the increment in $y$ over a step, including:\n\n- the modified Euler method, which predicts using Euler's method and then corrects that prediction, and\n\n- the RK4 method, which combines several slope evaluations within a single step.\n\nAnother group of methods, called the **Adams-Bashforth-Moulton methods**, takes a different approach. Rather than computing multiple slopes at new points, these methods reuse slopes from previous steps in a predictor-corrector framework, giving high accuracy with relatively little additional work.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on one of the simplest: the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the **$2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method** (**ABM $2$-step**) with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ where $x_{-1} = x-\\Delta x.$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nSince ABM $2$-step uses the slope from the previous step, it cannot be started from a single initial condition alone. \nInstead, we initialize the method by computing the first step with a one-step method, typically the RK4 method.\n\nOnce the prediction $y_p$ is computed, we apply a corrector equation to refine our approximation. We'll return to this step shortly.\nBefore that, let's see an example of how to apply the predictor equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a2f37b-38d8-5a6f-bc13-9086af674ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14412 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5af445aa-9ec9-5489-9176-c4332bff0a03" :step/math-academy-id 611738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222510 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |  |  |\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c403981-071d-5b27-8ff8-99c1ea98715b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14615 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "fb2bf195-d713-5c11-b38c-0b93be4ea2b8" :step/math-academy-id 611739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221161 :question/id #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308373}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "183435e5-3131-5485-8b09-dfbc5b6bc6b9" :step/math-academy-id 611740 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222479 :tutorial/content "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe **$2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton** (**ABM $2$-step**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nThe final approximation for the new step is $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y.$\n\nSo, the corrector of the ABM $2$-step combines the slope evaluated at the predicted point with the slope evaluated at the current point and the previous point. Let's demonstrate how to apply the corrector equation to our previous example." :tutorial/id #uuid "684ff8c6-ca91-5e58-82e6-41d645b1e6db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14372 :tutorial/title "The Two-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method"} :step/id #uuid "78553a6e-4fa7-55a6-9f7b-b53ad1a3a237" :step/math-academy-id 611743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "098a5bb7-0ece-59ec-8400-a8e3efe1b00a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14385 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "702af9a1-f869-585a-9d62-8b4e15161407" :step/math-academy-id 611744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221164 :question/id #uuid "a185d6d7-e351-5f24-891a-aeb318cef584" :question/math-academy-id "e-21565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374683}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"} :step/id #uuid "f8c031d4-df22-535d-926c-b13ae32837bd" :step/math-academy-id 611745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221160 :question/id #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" :question/math-academy-id "e-21538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374711}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "a7c6fe9a-69d3-5418-8b88-993af0fa999e" :step/math-academy-id 611748 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221165 :question/id #uuid "ab9a026d-ddb2-52e8-9f52-4fbcf9b482a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398774}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "40865e2e-2c40-5544-acb9-196c8c726a9f" :step/math-academy-id 611751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222485 :tutorial/content "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2})|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from [math]0[/math] to [math]1.[/math]\n\nNotice that evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta = 0,[/math] we have\n\\begin{align*}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSimilarly, evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta=-1[/math], we get\n\\begin{align*}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]y'_{-1}[/math] is the slope at the previous point [math](x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).[/math]\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a *linear* interpolant using its values at [math]\\theta=0[/math] and [math]\\theta=-1.[/math]\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n\\begin{align*}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "dee56e00-8f80-598d-a5ea-b99a6322bddd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14478 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AB2 Predictor"} :step/id #uuid "d6baca6f-a078-54e6-b8c4-a3e52eb82476" :step/math-academy-id 611754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222486 :tutorial/content "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwe had $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x)).\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$ \n\n- For $\\theta = 0,$ we have $g(0) = y'.$\n\n- For $\\theta = -1,$ we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}.$\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$ But, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to $\\theta = 1{:}$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p).$\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p).\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta +1){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\theta -1)(\\theta +1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta -1){y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}q(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'})\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\frac{1}{3}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{-1}^{'}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{y}_{p}^{'}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n\\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n= \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aa97723-f737-5f47-be29-c04919e09c0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14479 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AM2 Corrector"} :step/id #uuid "5990a7fa-57e1-5d5e-9ae3-587f865f923b" :step/math-academy-id 611755}] :activity/title "The ABM2 Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221477}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180488} :activity/id #uuid "081747a0-57ee-5aec-8eb5-e43238a80d3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916 :topic/id #uuid "cbfc5fbb-c887-5448-b39f-e7818405fa55" :topic/math-academy-id 3711} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180718 :tutorial/content "The line integral of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf F$ along a piecewise smooth curve $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r .\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from an initial point $A$ to a terminal point $B$ along the curve.\n\nIf the curve can be parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then we can calculate the line integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm dt\n$$\n\nOften, the trickiest part is finding a parameterization for $C.$ In this lesson, we'll consider several different cases" :tutorial/id #uuid "ec010a30-571c-5ad5-b30a-c18ffdd997e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5823 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b85eebf-c70c-58a0-9476-5bcb73727ad3" :step/math-academy-id 35690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414926}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"} :step/id #uuid "c535e9dc-5505-59aa-ae55-5a85fcb5135c" :step/math-academy-id 35691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179598 :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414927}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"} :step/id #uuid "922dce6d-b939-516c-95ed-a47bcba850e1" :step/math-academy-id 35692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180489}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179599 :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414928}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"} :step/id #uuid "2bd70267-cee5-535e-880d-6ade2c843224" :step/math-academy-id 35693 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180490}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over General Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179864}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199393} :activity/id #uuid "f3742955-3684-5655-938c-85f92fc58b37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198088 :question/id #uuid "ed6483ae-971a-543b-adb6-72ac5809b602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410196}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "81974eea-7a73-591a-8f79-590de81df808" :step/math-academy-id 45752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199515 :tutorial/content "If $A$ is the matrix corresponding to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ then:  \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$ of $A,$ and the maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$ of $A,$ and the minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSuppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-10\\: \\! x_1x_2+x_2^2\n$$\n\n \n\nwhen $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is $6.$ Let's use this to find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ where this maximum value is attained. \n\nSo first, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is indeed the vector we were looking for: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{\\text{max}}) & ={(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}-10(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{10}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ae6a7-1f29-5274-bd82-d2d8acfef6b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7954 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "08dd4ae6-a6ab-54da-a4ca-840835781b76" :step/math-academy-id 45755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198089 :question/id #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12663"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050447}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410198}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "47cdbdea-03d3-506b-a42b-a5ac82733039" :step/math-academy-id 45756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198090 :question/id #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "d2f76172-74c1-501b-a19c-68e6454733cb" :step/math-academy-id 45757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199516 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_1x_2+x_2^2,\n$$\n\n over the ellipse $4x_1^2+x_2^2=4$ equals $5.$ How can we find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which this maximum is attained?\n\nWe can find this vector by applying a suitable change of variable. This change of variable should transform our problem into one where we need to maximize a related quadratic form $Q'$ on the unit circle. Let's see how this is done.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_1^2}+ \\dfrac{x_2^2}{2^2}=1\n$$\n\nwhich  represents an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$$\ny_1={x_1}, \\qquad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{2},\n$$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}=Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in the new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the goal is to maximize $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ which we do using our usual methods.  \n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\max} = 5.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e750f422-509a-598a-858a-c11360c80eea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7955 :tutorial/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms Over Ellipses"} :step/id #uuid "f3b4db0d-8fec-573f-8597-7e9b0d0a4474" :step/math-academy-id 45758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199547 :tutorial/content "To complete the picture, let's prove the main theorem of this lesson:\n\n> *The maximum value of a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$*\n\n**Note:** We'll show only the first part (about the maximum value) since a similar argument can be used to prove the second part (about the minimum value).\n\nLet $A$ be a real $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix corresponding to a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin.$ As a result, there must be an *orthonormal* matrix $P$ such that \n\n- $D=P^{-1}AP=P^TAP,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\n- The diagonal entries of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2 \\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,$ where $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = \\lambda_1.$\n\n- The columns $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\mathbf{u}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{u}_n$ of $P$ form an orthonormal set of unit eigenvectors of $A.$ So, $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}} = \\mathbf{u}_1.$\n\nTake $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y.$ Since, $P$ is orthonormal, we have\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\|P \\mathbf y \\| = \\|\\mathbf y\\|\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf x^T\\!A\\mathbf x = (P\\mathbf y)^T\\!A(P\\mathbf y) = \\mathbf{y}^T (P^T\\!AP) \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}^T D \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n- $\\|\\mathbf x\\| =1 \\Longleftrightarrow \\|\\mathbf y\\| =1$ for all $\\mathbf y$ such that $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y$\n\n- Restricted to the unit sphere of $\\Bbb R^n,$ the images of the quadratic forms $Q(\\mathbf x)$  and $Q'(\\mathbf y)= \\mathbf y^T D\\mathbf y$ coincide: \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1\\}= \\{\\mathbf y^TD\\mathbf y: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf y\\|=1\\}  \\subset \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any unit vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={Q}^{'}(y) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{1}{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{2}{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{n}{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{y}_{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,‖y{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that  that \n\n$$\nM = \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{y} \\| = 1 \\} \\le \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, if we take $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & … & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $Q(P\\mathbf{e}_1) = Q'(\\mathbf{e}_1) = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ So, we conclude that $M = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathbf{u}_1 = P\\mathbf{e}_1$ we have that \n$Q(\\mathbf u_1) =  \\mathbf e_1^T D \\mathbf e_1 = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ \nTherefore, the maximum value is attained at $\\mathbf u_{\\textrm{max}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "364005f5-f0fc-5d17-9d6b-288d52abe715" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8176 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Main Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3a8e9986-4b85-5793-91b8-d47f73b67205" :step/math-academy-id 54038}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198510}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410202} :activity/id #uuid "f2727b60-7a6b-5f3a-a237-6a67bfb8139d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410167} {:answer/id #uuid "56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410174} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410178} {:answer/id #uuid "6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138128" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d7e6a3a-4e90-51f7-9e6d-1f36e15dea08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410172} {:answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410173} {:answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410185} {:answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3781c4aa-7e7b-5b51-9af4-d354711b0091"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410166} {:answer/id #uuid "1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} {:answer/id #uuid "31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410171} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410177} {:answer/id #uuid "a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170506" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80ab5b9e-fd44-5cd4-b555-7638c14ed974" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410175} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410180} {:answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410183} {:answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f589cb-239b-530f-99ce-e58d907d8d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-136828" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0403e816-9a8a-5caa-a60f-9e0db37c2581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410165} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410170} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc00fe-d9dc-5e75-8939-93c74926f323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410182} {:answer/id #uuid "c87e6493-0b51-5dfe-8402-a4a84620be0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410187} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e17c0-a680-5fd1-840a-1550ea0d3fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59381183-ff09-50a9-9c38-82023491a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0bc9465-a035-5136-b09d-b009ddf817a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170400" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f08afe40-e4fb-52cb-a8c1-acffe9c0cb58" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410201}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410164}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414677} :activity/id #uuid "dc255669-05a8-5b16-b22c-2690d4b2f343" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f3ea30-3f9d-5508-ab40-ee2dba4f0888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281708} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5452df-294d-5701-b4af-bbf16d6e0530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282056} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d984c-3905-5e24-bf07-c5dd11a46a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2908d2-0e8d-5999-a64a-a03cd4b68ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eb5798-946a-56c8-9937-6c6a91e2207e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9ad5-298c-5e24-9d44-dc14f057d818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80991c77-0c00-52ac-bae2-41a3b83ff730" :question/math-academy-id "q-170515" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14546d02-e8ca-5bbd-82f2-37c20dd8c152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410172} {:answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410173} {:answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410185} {:answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "310e7c10-e8a2-5719-93d8-e4b398b84456"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fcc9d7-da26-566b-892f-8ca0dccbf100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389631} {:answer/id #uuid "a86affbe-00af-5daa-9d5d-d6b4c732cb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389632} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b263a-4cb7-517f-88b9-8ca97ec6707e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389633} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f8b750-04ea-55ad-b825-30a6ece0925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389634} {:answer/id #uuid "0ceac102-859c-568b-8eb7-f0592356b505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389631} :answer-field/id #uuid "205f15e5-0108-5b4a-888b-1b37b338482c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8daa1f82-58aa-5738-bbe7-9b509869abcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-170411" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5eb99804-d9c9-598f-850b-ee8266ef1130" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1512bf0f-a0b6-5737-8456-15bf7348d13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414668} {:answer/id #uuid "189d044b-20ef-594f-9050-30d36ea1c805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414669} {:answer/id #uuid "3cba96ef-b9b9-582c-9ce3-fb19b45e7859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414670} {:answer/id #uuid "b75669b6-5915-5577-8d99-85ede9b169dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414671} {:answer/id #uuid 